{
  "raw": {
    "https://www.macalester.edu/provost/wp-content/uploads/sites/268/2026/08/Behavioral-Economics-202627rev.pdf": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor – Behavioral Economics",
      "description": "Assistant Professor – Behavioral Economics.  \n \nAssistant Professor \nBehavioral Economics \n \nThe Department of Economics at Macalester College invites applications for a full-time \ntenure track position at the Assistant Professor level to begin in the 2027-28 academic \nyear. We seek candidates with teaching and research interests in Behavioral Economics. \nMacalester College values a strong commitment to research, a promise of successful \nscholarly engagement, and dedication to teaching excellence in a liberal arts environment. \nA Ph.D. degree is expected by August 2027. \nAt Macalester College, diversity, equity, and inclusion are central to our educational \nmission and values. We are committed to the institutional change required for visioning \nand realizing a more equitable and socially just college and society. In this spirit, we pride \nourselves on our inclusive curriculum and pedagogy (e.g. active learning, real world \napplications, early engagement in research).  \nMacalester also provides an attractive environment for economists. We offer a vibrant, \nengaged and collegial environment in the heart of the Twin Cities. We currently offer the \nlargest single major on campus, and have a track record of exceptional undergraduate \nresearch collaboration. Macalester faculty teach five semester course sections per year \n(typically two to three distinct courses), supervise honors theses, maintain an active \nscholarly agenda, and engage in departmental activities. During their first year, new faculty \nteach four courses and attend a weekly new faculty development seminar.  \nFor full consideration, please submit the following materials to Academic Jobs Online by \nNovember 13, 2026  (https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32416):  \n1. Curriculum vitae (CV),  \n2. Job market paper, \n3. Graduate school transcripts (no GPA requirement), \n4. Three letters of recommendation – at least one of which discusses your potential as \na teacher,  \n5. Teaching statement: a statement of your experience in, commitment to, and vision \nfor teaching undergraduate students. Discuss pedagogical approaches (e.g., inclusive \nteaching, active and innovative teaching strategies, assessment of learning \noutcomes), future teaching development goals, and courses you’d be passionate to \nteach or introduce into the curriculum,  \n6. A cover letter (2-3 pages) that provides an executive summary of your: \na. specific interest in joining the Economics Department at Macalester College, \nb. training and experience in Economics or a closely related field, \nc. contributions to the scholarly field and future research goals, \nd. demonstrated activity and commitment to inclusive pedagogies, scholarship, \nservice, and/or community engagement;  \ne. experience and vision regarding teaching, advising, and mentorship of \nstudents with a variety of experiences and backgrounds with attention to \nstudents who experience structural barriers and limited access to \neducational opportunities. \nApplication materials should be written to be accessible to all members of the search \ncommittee (including Economics and non-Economics faculty and undergraduate students), \nas well as the college Provost and Dean of the Faculty. \nThe starting salary range for this position will be $99,000-$102,000, depending on \nexperience. If the candidate is an advanced assistant candidate, salary would be above this \nrange and based on internal equity. At Macalester, we offer a generous package of \nemployee benefits. Here are some highlights: \n• 10% employer contributions to the retirement plan. \n• Comprehensive health insurance options, including a high-deductible health plan \nand PPO plan \n• Generous employer contribution to Health Savings Account. \n• Tuition assistance which pays between 75% - 100% of eligible tuition for employee \ndependents at select institutions. \n• Free primary care for most services through Nice Healthcare when enrolled in a \nMacalester health plan. \n• Access to the athletics fitness facility, wellness coaching, and activities. \nYou may see our full benefits options on our Benefits Page. \nMacalester College is a distinctive, private liberal arts college in the vibrant Minneapolis-\nSaint Paul metropolitan area, with a population of approximately three million people and \nhome to numerous colleges and universities, including the University of Minnesota. \nMacalester's diverse student body comprises over 2000 undergraduates from 49 states, the \nDistrict of Columbia, and 107 countries. The College maintains a longstanding commitment \nto academic distinction with a special emphasis on global engagement, justice and equity, \nand service to society. Macalester College invites candidates who enrich our community \nand strongly encourages applications across a variety of experiences and identities. \n \n \nPlease note that candidates who are invited for a campus interview will be required to \ncomplete faculty screening questions, and offers of employment are contingent upon a \nsuccessful criminal background check at the time of offer. Additionally, finalist candidates \nwill be asked to provide reference contacts and we may call for additional references. \nMacalester College does not discriminate on the grounds of sex, gender identity, race, color, \nnational origin, age, religion, creed, disability, marital status, familial status, pregnancy, \ngenetic information, membership or activity in a local human rights commission, veteran \nstatus, sexual orientation, status with regard to public assistance, or other categories \nprotected by law. For additional information, see Macalester College's full Notice of \nNondiscrimination. \nCONTACT: Amy Damon, Chair, Department of Economics: adamon@macalester.edu. \n "
    },
    "https://www.macalester.edu/provost/wp-content/uploads/sites/268/2026/08/Art_Art-History-Visiting-Faculty-Position-Posting-for-AY26-27.pdf": {
      "title": "Visiting Faculty – Art and Art History",
      "description": "Visiting Faculty – Art and Art History.  \n \nPosting Date: 8/4/2026 \nArt/Art History Visiting Faculty Position Posting for AY26-27 \nTitle: Visiting Faculty \nDepartment: Art and Art History \nTerm: 2026-2027 Academic Year \nThe Macalester College Department of Art and Art History invites applications for a three-\ncourse-equivalent (.58FTE) non-tenure-track visiting position in the 2026-2027 academic \nyear. The successful candidate will teach the second half of two four-credit Fall 2026 term \ncourses (“Sculpture 1” and “Soft Sculpture and the Fiber Arts”) and two four-credit \ncourses (“Sculpture 1” and “3D Design”) in the Spring 2027 term. The fall courses have set \ndays and times of MWF 9:40-11:40am and MW 1:10-4:20pm, and the position would \nbegin in mid to late October 2026. The days and times of the spring semester classes have \nyet to be scheduled, and there will be some flexibility for the successful candidate. \nAcademic year calendar information is available at \nhttps://www.macalester.edu/registrar/academic-calendars/  \nA terminal degree (MFA) in a 3D studio art field is required. Visa sponsorship is not \navailable for this position. In addition, candidates relying on an OPT Visa with a STEM \nextension are not eligible for hire.  \nCompensation and Benefits: \nThe starting salary range for this position will be $26,000-$27,000 and will depend upon \nteaching experience and highest degree attained.  \nAll visiting faculty members will have access to the athletics fitness facility, wellness \ncoaching, and campus activities. \nTo apply, please email curriculum vitae, cover letter, and a list of three references \n(including contact information) to the Art and Art History Department Chair, Megan \nVossler (mvossler@macalester.edu). Complete applications received by August 23, 2026 \nwill be given full consideration. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue \nuntil the position is filled. For further information about the position (including time \ncommitment and workload expectations), contact Megan Vossler. \nAt Macalester College, equity and inclusion are central to our educational mission and \nvalues. We are committed to preparing students to be agents of change who create a more \njust and peaceful world. Strong candidates have a demonstrated commitment to teaching \nstudents from diverse backgrounds and recognize that structural barriers limit access to \neducational opportunities.  \n \nMacalester College is a highly selective, private liberal arts college in the vibrant \nMinneapolis-Saint Paul metropolitan area, with a population of approximately three \nmillion people and home to numerous colleges and universities, including the University \nof Minnesota. Macalester College is located in Saint Paul on the ancestral homeland of the \nDakota people who were forcibly exiled because of aggressive and persistent settler \ncolonialism. Macalester’s diverse student body comprises over 2000 undergraduates \n(15% international and 32% US students of color), who hail from 45 states and over 70 \nnations. The College maintains a longstanding commitment to academic excellence, with a \nspecial emphasis on internationalism, multiculturalism, and service to society.  As an \nEqual Opportunity employer supportive of affirmative efforts to achieve diversity among \nits faculty, Macalester College strongly encourages applications from women, non-binary \npeople, and members of historically underrepresented minority groups. \n "
    },
    "https://www.macalester.edu/provost/wp-content/uploads/sites/268/2026/10/Chemistry-spring-27.pdf": {
      "title": "Visiting Faculty – Chemistry",
      "description": "Visiting Faculty – Chemistry.  \n \nVisiting Faculty in Chemistry \n  \nThe Macalester College Department of Chemistry invites applications for a three-course-\nequivalent (.58FTE) non-tenure-track visiting position in the 2027 Spring Term. The \nsuccessful candidate will teach three sections of CHEM 112 - General Chemistry II: \nEnergetics and Reactivity. Academic year calendar information is available at \nhttps://www.macalester.edu/registrar/academic-calendars/  \nA terminal degree is desirable but not required. Visa sponsorship is not available for this \nposition. In addition, candidates relying on an OPT Visa with a STEM extension are not \neligible for hire.  \nCompensation and Benefits: \nThe starting salary range for this position will be $26,000-$27,000 and will depend upon \nteaching experience and highest degree attained. This is a part-time benefits eligible \nposition with a benefit start date of February 1, 2027. \nAll visiting faculty members will have access to the athletics fitness facility, wellness \ncoaching, and campus activities. \nTo apply, please email curriculum vitae, cover letter that describes your interests and \nqualifications, and a list of three references (including contact information) to the Chair of \nthe Chemistry Department,  Kathryn Splan (splank@macalester.edu). Review of \napplications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. For further \ninformation about the position (including time commitment and workload expectations), \ncontact Kathryn Splan. \nAt Macalester College, equity and inclusion are central to our educational mission and \nvalues. We are committed to preparing students to be agents of change who create a more \njust and peaceful world. Strong candidates have a demonstrated commitment to teaching \nstudents from diverse backgrounds and recognize that structural barriers limit access to \neducational opportunities.  \nMacalester College is a highly selective, private liberal arts college in the vibrant \nMinneapolis-Saint Paul metropolitan area, with a population of approximately three \nmillion people and home to numerous colleges and universities, including the University \nof Minnesota. Macalester College is located in Saint Paul on the ancestral homeland of the \nDakota people who were forcibly exiled because of aggressive and persistent settler \n \n \ncolonialism. Macalester’s diverse student body comprises over 2000 undergraduates \n(15% international and 32% US students of color), who hail from 45 states and over 70 \nnations. The College maintains a longstanding commitment to academic excellence, with a \nspecial emphasis on internationalism, multiculturalism, and service to society.  As an \nEqual Opportunity employer supportive of affirmative efforts to achieve diversity among \nits faculty, Macalester College strongly encourages applications from women, non-binary \npeople, and members of historically underrepresented minority groups. "
    },
    "https://www.macalester.edu/provost/wp-content/uploads/sites/268/2026/10/German-27.pdf": {
      "title": "Visiting Faculty – German and Russian Studies – German",
      "description": "Visiting Faculty – German and Russian Studies – German.  \n \nVisiting Faculty in German and Russian Studies \n  \nThe Macalester College Department of German and Russian Studies invites applications \nfor a full-time (1.00 FTE), non-tenure-track visiting position in German language, \nliterature, and culture, to begin Fall 2027. This position is renewable for a second year, \npending review of teaching. The teaching load per year is five courses, four in the \nlanguage sequence and one taught in English and cross-listed with an allied department \nor program. Specialization is open; however, candidates should be able to teach a wide \nvariety of German language and culture courses.  \nA PhD in German or a related field (in hand or near completion), native or near-native \ncommand of German, significant experience and demonstrated excellence in teaching \nGerman at multiple levels, and a focus on student recruitment and program building are \nessential. A robust research program is desired. The successful candidate will be an \nenthusiastic and supportive participant in departmental co-curricular activities, ranging \nfrom Kaffeestunde and Trivia to student performances. \nVisa sponsorship is not available for this position. In addition, candidates relying on an \nOPT Visa with a STEM extension are not eligible for hire.  \nCompensation and Benefits: \nThe starting salary range for this position will be $63,000-$65,000 and will depend upon \nteaching experience and highest degree attained.  \nAt Macalester, we offer a generous package of employee benefits. Here are some \nhighlights: \n• 10% employer contributions to the retirement plan. \n• Comprehensive health insurance options, including a high-deductible health plan \nand PPO plan. \n• Generous employer contribution to Health Savings Account. \n• Tuition assistance which pays between 75% - 100% of eligible tuition for \nemployee dependents at select institutions. \n• Free primary care for most services through Nice Healthcare when enrolled in a \nMacalester health plan. \n• Access to the athletics fitness facility, wellness coaching, and activities. \n \n \nYou may see our full benefits options on our Benefits Page. \nTo apply, electronically submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, evidence of teaching \nexcellence, two letters of recommendation, and a writing sample to \nhttps://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/33166. We encourage candidates to discuss in \ntheir cover letter how they see themselves as aligned with Macalester's mission and \nstatement of purpose and belief. Review of applications will begin November 23, 2026 \nand continue until the position is filled. Questions should be directed to the chair of the \nsearch committee, Professor David Martyn at martyn@macalester.edu. \nAt Macalester College, equity and inclusion are central to our educational mission and \nvalues. We are committed to preparing students to be agents of change who create a more \njust and peaceful world. Strong candidates have a demonstrated commitment to teaching \nstudents from diverse backgrounds and recognize that structural barriers limit access to \neducational opportunities.  \nMacalester College is a highly selective, private liberal arts college in the vibrant \nMinneapolis-Saint Paul metropolitan area, with a population of approximately three \nmillion people and home to numerous colleges and universities, including the University \nof Minnesota. Macalester College is located in Saint Paul on the ancestral homeland of the \nDakota people who were forcibly exiled because of aggressive and persistent settler \ncolonialism. Macalester’s diverse student body comprises over 2000 undergraduates \n(15% international and 32% US students of color), who hail from 45 states and over 70 \nnations. The College maintains a longstanding commitment to academic excellence, with a \nspecial emphasis on internationalism, multiculturalism, and service to society.  As an \nEqual Opportunity employer supportive of affirmative efforts to achieve diversity among \nits faculty, Macalester College strongly encourages applications from women, non-binary \npeople, and members of historically underrepresented minority groups. "
    }
  },
